# Status

Where everything stands, one file per thing that ships. Open the file for the
thing you care about; this page is the one-line version of each.

| Tool | Covers | Where it stands | Next |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| [**language**](lang.hmd) | The format itself — grammar, resolution, frontmatter, diagnostics, publication | Specified and implemented twice, with the specification a single normative text | Decide whether headings written with an underline are addressable, since the tools and the website currently disagree |
| [**hmd**](hmd.hmd) | The canonical implementation: the CLI, the core, the site plugin, the package | Complete and released; everything the format specifies is implemented | Property-based tests of the resolver, over generated vaults |
| [**@hypermarkdown/core**](ts-core.hmd) | The format in TypeScript, held to the canonical implementation by a shared corpus | `0.1.1` on npm; complete except the card publication *model*, and byte-identical to the canonical linter on everything it implements | Port the publication model, once the specification says which proposal owns it here |
| [**hypermarkdown (Rust)**](rs-core.hmd) | The resolver as a Rust library crate, held to the canonical implementation by the shared corpus | Written, passing every corpus case, and published to crates.io at 0.1.0 | A release workflow, and HQL taking it as its resolver |
| [**hmd-lsp**](lsp.hmd) | The language server, as a Rust program on the resolver crate: tokens, definition, hover, and three diagnostics | Written and tested, with no release and no editor using it yet | A release workflow, and the toolchain carrying the binary |
| [**hmd-semantics**](semantics.hmd) | The builder of a vault's semantic index: card text embedded into an SQLite file in the vault's `.hmd/` | Moved here from HQL and passing its tests; builds the card itself at one depth, and not published | The two summary depths and the schema version that carries them |
| [**VS Code extension**](vsc-ext.hmd) | The hmd preview, diagnostics, and the graph, inside the editor | `0.3.0` live on both galleries, and the preview label is off | Automate the by-hand release checks |
| [**website**](pages.hmd) | <https://hypermarkdown.org> — the book, the prose, the branding, the deploy | Live and building clean | The roadmap page, and the stale state claims the audit for it turned up |

## Generated views

Three cards here are written by queries, not by hand. Each one is an HQL cell
over the cards of `doc/`, which is a vault for that reason
([`doc/.hmd/config.toml`](../.hmd/config.toml)), with its output saved under
the cell:

| View | Reads | Shows |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [`kanban.hmd`](kanban.hmd) | Each issue's `**Column**:` line, and `doc/issues/archive/` | The lanes, the board, and the archive |
| [`proposals.hmd`](proposals.hmd) | Each proposal's title and `**Status**:` line | Every proposal, its status, and the trackers that cite it |
| [`open.hmd`](open.hmd) | The **Open** table of every tracker | Every open row, by state, with the tracker that owns it |

They are derived reads, not trackers: the trackers stay the only place work is
recorded, and the views only gather it. Never edit an output by hand. Re-render
in the same commit that moves an issue, changes a proposal's status, or edits
an Open row, from the repository root:

```sh
hql render --in-place --emit after doc/status/kanban.hmd doc/status/proposals.hmd doc/status/open.hmd
```

This needs `hql` 0.8.0 or later. Earlier versions do not read `location_path`
from the vault's configuration and refuse to run. The pinned toolchain ships
0.8.0 since toolchain 0.5.3 (`node scripts/toolchain.mjs` prints its
directory), and an `hql` installed with `cargo install hql` works too.

## Rows as data

[`hmd.hmd`](hmd.hmd) keeps its rows as data, and HQL cells write its tables.
It is the first tracker to do so; the other seven still write their tables by
hand.

The rows are a list named `work` in the card's frontmatter. Each row has
these fields:

| Field | Holds |
| --- | --- |
| `kind` | `open`, `broken`, `limitation`, `done`, or `question`: the section the row belongs to |
| `state` | `todo`, `doing`, `backlog`, `blocked`, or `parked`, on `open` and `broken` rows |
| `tags` | the parts of the tool the row is about, such as `linter`, `site`, or `lsp` |
| `text` | the row itself, as Markdown |
| `notes` | the second column: the notes, the symptom, or why a limitation stands |
| `refs` | the references that back the row |

To change a row, edit it in the frontmatter and re-render the card with the
command under [Generated views](#generated-views), adding `doc/status/hmd.hmd`
to it. Never edit a table under a cell by hand. The rendered tables are part
of the card, so `hmd lint` checks the references in them.

Each section of the tracker is one cell that filters `work` by `kind`. The
**By tag** section counts the rows that carry each tag. A query in any other
card can read the same rows from `cards`, as [`open.hmd`](open.hmd) does.

## How these files work

Each tracker reads in one order, and it is the order a reader asks in:

1. **Status** — what is happening, in prose. No tables, no identifiers.
2. **Open** — what is not done, each row marked `todo`, `doing`, `backlog`,
   `blocked`, or `parked`, with blocked rows naming what unblocks them.
3. **Broken** — defects, each with the symptom you would actually see.
4. **Limitations (known gaps)** — accepted, not being fixed, each with why it
   stands. A defect is a promise to fix; a limitation is a decision not to.
5. **Done** — last, one line per shipped capability. The detail lives in each
   tool's `CHANGELOG.md`.

Then the gates that verify it, the open questions, and a changelog of edits to
the tracker itself.

**A row says what it is, not where it came from.** Anything a reader would have
to look up — a rule identifier, an issue number — trails the sentence in
brackets and never replaces it. Nothing here should require opening another file
to understand.

**Every table carries a `References` column, and it is the last one.** It holds
the references that back the row — a reference names its target rather than
numbering it, so `[[hmd-0021#10-deferred-tabs]]` and never a section sign and a
number. A row with nothing genuinely behind it carries `—` rather than a
plausible guess. This is the column that replaced an earlier `Spec` column
answering "which proposal section": the question was never wrong, the answer
was, because a number tells a reader where something currently sits and that is
the one fact guaranteed to change. These references are ordinary links to a
heading, as the specification defines them under *Heading anchors*; the
trackers are cards, so `hmd lint` checks every one of these references.

A tracker cites a proposal under a short name, such as `hmd-0021`. The
tracker's frontmatter binds that name with an import:
`from /proposals/HMD-0021 import index as hmd-0021`. Add an import when a
tracker cites a proposal for the first time. The fragment is matched against
the proposal's headings after the language normalizes both, and punctuation
becomes a separator, so the heading `3.1 Masking` is `#3-1-masking` and `The
extension's identity` is `#the-extension-s-identity`.

**Update the tracker in the same commit that changes the code.** A tracker
updated afterwards describes a repository that no longer exists, and one updated
in advance describes one that does not exist yet.

These are not the board. [`doc/issues/`](../issues/) carries work in flight, one
numbered card each; a card picked up becomes a row here, and the row is the
durable half. Proposals under [`doc/proposals/`](../proposals/) record decisions
and carry no trackers of their own.
